d review -> land -> H3-1.\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:167:Done: (1) Muse round 3 (subscription, shell-free) died on 429 \"Subscription quota exhausted\" (resets 2026-11-03 13:41 UTC = 20:41 +07 tomorrow) — it did leave the declarations file the brief asked for, preserved for the next round. Round 4 on \"the API lane\" died identically: the API key is NOT in this seat's environment and muse's auth store holds ONLY the meta OAuth subscription (no `muse auth set` ever ran) — the reference's API-overflow lane needs the owner to supply the key. Mistral still key-broken (401). (2) With muse dead until tomorrow and mistral parked, implementation resumed on codex: the reset (~22:05 +07) removed the quota basis of the closure; the brief's muse-specific shell-free section was replaced with codex constraints (focused verification allowed, no push, review-before-landing) and dispatched as round 5 (log collab/1793790000000-engineer-h3-0-declared-dispositions.codex-r2.stdout.log). The round is alive and editing test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj. (3) Routing-status updated with the full credential/quota picture.\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:171:Next: round 5 returns -> run the full oracle myself -> reviewer round (kimi or codex-fresh) -> architect (kimi) disposition-guard review -> land -> H3-1.\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:182:Next: codex round 5 returns -> full oracle verification by the seat -> kimi code-quality review (the muse/mistral gate applied to the review where useful) -> compiler + disposition-guard reviews -> land -> H3-1 to mistral.\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:193:Next: vibe returns -> the seat runs the full oracle + its reported checks -> kimi code-quality review -> compiler review of the classifier -> architect (kimi) disposition-guard review -> land -> H3-1 (also vibe).\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:211:Done: (1) With no answer to the lane question, the seat implemented H3-0 directly per the recommendation. Reading the pristine harness revealed the truth the delegates had missed: the ENTIRE H3-0 machinery already exists at HEAD — the classifier `kernel-host-value-deviation` with exact guards and negative-control tests (including the forged-descriptor rejection), `kernel-row` recording `:recorded-deviation` statuses, and the writer emitting `:recorded-portability-boundaries` from classified rows. The SINGLE root cause of the whole regression is astra's r1 finding: the classifier's `(values/closure? meta)` is representation-stale — the reconciled compiler's guest closure at :meta is the admitted §8.10 descriptor map (measured: clojure.lang.PersistentArrayMap with :clj/type :function, :yang.call/entry, :clj/admission), so the check fails, deviations come back empty, the writer honestly records none, and the 2285 assertion fails. The vibe rounds \"fixed\" everything EXCEPT this (round 2 even replaced the working check with an invented shape). (2) The seat's implementation: `kernel-callable-descriptor?` validating the genuine descriptor (function type marker + closure entry + closure admission witness — codebase-true shapes, not invented ones); the classifier accepts either representation; the versioned declarations file drives boundary/upstream-value/reason with revalidation guards (declared message == live message, declared script == live script, normalized); negative controls extended (both representations classify; forgeries — missing admission, non-closure entry, wrong type marker, invented shape — refuse; a mutated declaration fails via with-redefs); the writer gained a `dir` overload so the round-trip test exercises the REAL writer against a temp directory replaying the checked-in evidence (no fixture recompute). The muse round's declarations file was retained as-is (kimi had retained it).\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:214:Delegates: kimi (queued: quality review of the seat's diff + disposition-guard review).\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:222:Done: (1) After the owner left the lane question unanswered, the seat implemented H3-0 directly. Reading the pristine harness showed the ENTIRE machinery existed at HEAD — the single root cause was astra's r1 finding: the deviation classifier's `(values/closure? meta)` was representation-stale (the reconciled compiler publishes the admitted §8.10 descriptor at :meta — measured PersistentArrayMap). The vibe rounds had \"fixed\" everything except this one line's assumption. (2) The seat's implementation: `kernel-callable-descriptor?` (function marker + minted-closure entry + admission witness + true outcomes marker — codebase-true shapes); the classifier accepts both representations; the versioned slice9_declarations.edn (muse round 3's file, kimi-retained) drives boundary/upstream-value/reason with revalidation guards; the declarations contract validated at load (version/provenance/per-fixture exact boundaries/pinned revision — relabeling fails at load); negative controls extended (both representations classify; forgeries, payload/shape mutations, per-lane incompleteness, mutated calls and mutated declarations refuse); the writer gained a dir overload with a real-writer round-trip test; both narrative locations + the ledger row carry only the true accounting. (3) The kimi review pipeline (4 rounds, session_0f361a3a): r1 REVISE (5 concrete defects incl. the stale second gate.md section and my own \"H3-0 closed\" phrasing violation), r2 REVISE (copied-descriptor axis, shape-only control, 1689 staleness, declarations scope), r3 partial (scope pins accepted; copied-descriptor escalated), then the DISPOSITION-GUARD REVIEW: ACCEPT — the ruled question resolved as a responsibility split (the compiler owns descriptor authenticity with its own admission tests at 