{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream/ble.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.stream.ble\n  \"The raw Bluetooth layer of docs/design/dao.stream.ble.md: one radio\n   node exposed as dao.stream values. This layer carries bytes, peer\n   addresses and link facts and nothing else -- no decoding, retry,\n   ordering, dedup, fragmentation, reassembly, routing; each of those is\n   an interpreter above it, and the flooding mesh (dao.stream.ble.mesh)\n   is the first.\n\n   A radio node retains nothing, so it has no reader surface: a writer\n   handle (#{:writer :closable}) whose append! hands one addressed frame\n   to the radio, and a traffic stream, supplied by the composition, onto\n   which the host adapter deposits every received frame and every host\n   fact about the node and its links as one event. Readers hold their own\n   cursors on the traffic stream.\n\n   Binding is a host act, not create!, and the composition proceeds in\n   two explicit moves (section 3): the host seam (dao.stream.ble/dart)\n   takes {:identity id :deposit w :service-uuid u :max-frame-bytes n\n   :max-links n :announce-window-ms ms :now-ms f} -- a deposit writer and\n   nothing else, no function to invoke -- and returns at once {:send! f\n   :close! f}; the seam deposits :dao.stream.ble/bound with the node\n   identity on every host; the composition reads that event and calls\n   `writer` with the seam and the complete descriptor. A failed start\n   deposits :bind-failed and there is no writer.\n\n   A peer address is an opaque, link-scoped string -- Android reports a\n   MAC, iOS a per-local-device UUID -- and names one directional GATT\n   link as this radio's state observes it. No structure, no global\n   meaning, string equality: nothing above this layer may treat a peer\n   address as a node's name, which is why the mesh above floods (section\n   2 of the design).\n\n   A send failure is the caller's outcome when the host answers at call\n   time (:dao.stream/transport-error, clean: nothing was sent) and a\n   :dao.stream.ble/send-failed lifecycle event on the traffic stream for\n   its other observers -- always the event for a failure the host reports\n   only later, which is the channel this transport declares for an answer\n   it cannot give at call time (dao.stream.md, Writing). Excluded\n   append! outcomes: :full, because this layer holds no outbound queue\n   and a radio under pressure loses frames -- the medium's declared\n   nature, not a refusal; :refused, because no policy is composed in this\n   layer.\n\n   There is no :dao.stream/create entry. `attach!` on a BLE descriptor\n   off its host is :dao.stream/not-found; on its own host it resolves\n   only against nodes a composition chose to keep in its host-composed\n   attach closure -- `make-attacher` is that closure over a\n   composition-owned resolver, never a registry. Exposure is a real\n   hazard: a raw writer handle entered in a mirror table lets whoever\n   reaches it send arbitrary frames from this radio; no composition does\n   so without a gate on that entry (section 9).\"\n  (:require [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.base64 :as base64]))\n\n\n(def transport-type\n  \"The dao.stream/type of a bound radio node's descriptor.\"\n  :dao.stream/ble)\n\n\n(def default-service-uuid\n  \"The datom.world mesh profile's GATT service: a TX characteristic the\n   node notifies its frames on and an RX characteristic a central writes\n   its frames to. Composition data with this default; a composition that\n   changes it changes who can hear it.\"\n  \"7daf6c80-3f2a-4e11-9d47-1b2a3c4d5e6f\")\n\n\n(def default-tx-uuid\n  \"The mesh profile's TX characteristic (notify): this node's frames out\n   to a subscribed central.\"\n  \"7daf6c81-3f2a-4e11-9d47-1b2a3c4d5e6f\")\n\n\n(def default-rx-uuid\n  \"The mesh profile's RX characteristic (write): a central's frames in.\"\n  \"7daf6c82-3f2a-4e11-9d47-1b2a3c4d5e6f\")\n\n\n(def default-max-frame-bytes\n  \"The default symmetric frame budget: one notification payload under the\n   smallest ATT MTU hosts commonly negotiate (iOS has granted as little\n   as 185). A composition that observes a larger negotiated MTU may raise\n   it.\"\n  180)\n\n\n(def default-max-links\n  \"The default ceiling on concurrent GATT links: an iOS central holds\n   around ten.\"\n  8)\n\n\n(def default-announce-window-ms\n  \"The default discovery throttle: at most one peer-seen event per peer\n   per window, the latest RSSI winning.\"\n  2000)\n\n\n(defn- byte-length\n  [bs]\n  (bytes/length bs))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values (section 2)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn peer-address?\n  \"True iff `s` is a peer address: a non-empty string. Opaque by design --\n   no structure is assigned and none is checked.\"\n  [s]\n  (and (string? s) (pos? (count s))))\n\n\n(defn node-id?\n  \"True iff `s` is a node identity: a non-empty string, composition\n   minted, never authenticated by this layer.\"\n  [s]\n  (and (string? s) (pos? (count s))))\n\n\n(defn outbound?\n  \"True iff `v` is one outbound value of section 2: a destination peer\n   address and the frame as strict Base64. The decoded length against the\n   budget is the writer's check, not this one's.\"\n  [v]\n  (and (map? v)\n       (peer-address? (:dao.stream.ble/destination v))\n       (base64/text? (:dao.stream.ble/bytes v))))\n\n\n(defn frame-event?\n  \"True iff `v` is one received frame as an event: exact bytes as strict\n   Base64, and the source peer the radio observed.\"\n  [v]\n  (and (map? v)\n       (peer-address? (:dao.stream.ble/source v))\n       (base64/text? (:dao.stream.ble/bytes v))))\n\n\n(def lifecycle-events\n  \"The lifecycle events this layer declares. A transport-owned event\n   keyword outside this set is not this layer's.\"\n  #{:dao.stream.ble/bound\n    :dao.stream.ble/bind-failed\n    :dao.stream.ble/peer-seen\n    :dao.stream.ble/peer-lost\n    :dao.stream.ble/link-up\n    :dao.stream.ble/link-down\n    :dao.stream.ble/send-failed\n    :dao.stream.ble/closed})\n\n\n(defn lifecycle-event?\n  \"True iff `v` is one host fact about the node itself or its links.\"\n  [v]\n  (and (map? v)\n       (node-id? (:dao.stream.ble/node v))\n       (contains? lifecycle-events (:dao.stream.ble/event v))))\n\n\n(defn bound-event\n  \"The bound lifecycle event: the node exists, with its identity.\"\n  [id self]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/bound\n   :dao.stream.ble/self self})\n\n\n(defn bind-failed-event\n  \"The failed-bind lifecycle event. There is no writer.\"\n  [id reason]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/bind-failed\n   :dao.stream.ble/reason reason})\n\n\n(defn peer-seen-event\n  \"One observed advertisement of the service UUID, throttled by the seam\n   to one event per peer per :announce-window-ms, latest RSSI winning.\"\n  [id peer rssi]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/peer-seen\n   :dao.stream.ble/peer peer\n   :dao.stream.ble/rssi rssi})\n\n\n(defn peer-lost-event\n  \"The peer-lost lifecycle event: a link to the peer went down. S1 seams\n   deposit it only from link loss, never from advertisement silence --\n   silence is not a fact a radio reports.\"\n  [id peer]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/peer-lost\n   :dao.stream.ble/peer peer})\n\n\n(defn link-up-event\n  \"The link-up lifecycle event: one directional GATT link exists, the\n   role named (:central connected out, :peripheral connected in).\"\n  [id peer direction]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/link-up\n   :dao.stream.ble/peer peer\n   :dao.stream.ble/direction direction})\n\n\n(defn link-down-event\n  \"The link-down lifecycle event: the directional link named by the peer\n   address is gone.\"\n  [id peer]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/link-down\n   :dao.stream.ble/peer peer})\n\n\n(defn send-failed-event\n  \"The send-failure lifecycle event: the channel for a send failure the\n   host could not answer at call time, and the observers' copy of one it\n   could.\"\n  [id peer reason]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/send-failed\n   :dao.stream.ble/peer peer\n   :dao.stream.ble/reason reason})\n\n\n(defn closed-event\n  \"The closed lifecycle event, deposited while the deposit writer still\n   admits it.\"\n  [id]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/event :dao.stream.ble/closed})\n\n\n(defn frame-event\n  \"One received frame as an event: `bytes` encoded as Base64 so no host\n   byte array crosses onto a stream, with the source peer the radio\n   observed.\"\n  [id peer bytes]\n  {:dao.stream.ble/node id\n   :dao.stream.ble/source peer\n   :dao.stream.ble/bytes (base64/encode bytes)})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The descriptor (section 3)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn descriptor?\n  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ble :dao.stream/identity id\n     :dao.stream.ble/self <node identity>}\n   The descriptor names the local radio node. It is reachability data in\n   the honest, reduced sense: a BLE node is reachable by radio proximity\n   and by advertising the service UUID, never by dialing the descriptor.\"\n  [d]\n  (and (map? d)\n       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type d))\n       (node-id? (:dao.stream/identity d))\n       (node-id? (:dao.stream.ble/self d))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The writer handle (section 4)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn- check-outbound\n  \"One outbound value of section 2 as either {:bytes bs} -- the decoded\n   frame, ready for the radio -- or {:refusal reason}: a malformed\n   destination, bytes that are not strict Base64, or a decoded length\n   over the budget. Nothing was sent for a refusal.\"\n  [v max-frame-bytes]\n  (cond\n    (not (map? v))\n    {:refusal \"not an outbound value\"}\n\n    (not (peer-address? (:dao.stream.ble/destination v)))\n    {:refusal \"malformed destination\"}\n\n    (not (base64/text? (:dao.stream.ble/bytes v)))\n    {:refusal \"bytes are not strict Base64\"}\n\n    :else\n    (let [bs (base64/decode (:dao.stream.ble/bytes v))]\n      (if (> (byte-length bs) max-frame-bytes)\n        {:refusal \"decoded length over :max-frame-bytes\"}\n        {:bytes bs}))))\n\n\n(deftype BLEWriter\n  [send-fn close-fn the-descriptor max-frame-bytes closed]\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n\n  (descriptor\n    [_]\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n     :dao.stream/descriptor the-descriptor\n     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity the-descriptor)})\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n  (append!\n    [_ v]\n    (if @closed\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n      (let [checked (check-outbound v max-frame-bytes)]\n        (if-some [reason (:refusal checked)]\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value\n           :dao.stream.ble/reason reason}\n          (let [r (try\n                    (send-fn (:dao.stream.ble/destination v)\n                             (:bytes checked))\n                    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n                       :dao.stream.ble/reason (str e)}))]\n            (if (and (map? r) (contains? r :dao.stream/outcome))\n              (if (#{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/transport-error\n                     :dao.stream/closed} (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n                r\n                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error})\n              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n\n  (close!\n    [_]\n    (vreset! closed true)\n    (try (close-fn)\n         (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n           nil))\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))\n\n\n(defn writer\n  \"The composition's second move (section 3): the writer handle over a\n   bound `seam` ({:send! f :close! f}) and the complete `descriptor` the\n   bound event reported. `max-frame-bytes` is composition data, default\n   180, the same budget the seam was bound with.\"\n  ([seam descriptor]\n   (writer seam descriptor default-max-frame-bytes))\n  ([seam descriptor max-frame-bytes]\n   (when-not (and (map? seam)\n                  (fn? (:send! seam))\n                  (fn? (:close! seam)))\n     (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble seam is {:send! f :close! f}\"\n                     {:seam seam})))\n   (when-not (descriptor? descriptor)\n     (throw (ex-info \"not a dao.stream.ble descriptor\"\n                     {:descriptor descriptor})))\n   (BLEWriter. (:send! seam) (:close! seam) descriptor\n               (long max-frame-bytes) (volatile! false))))\n\n\n(defn make-attacher\n  \"The host-composed attach! closure over nodes a composition chose to\n   keep: `resolver` maps a node identity to its writer handle, or nil.\n   Attaching never creates, resolves nothing ambiently -- there is no\n   registry -- and the same handle is shared, not re-minted: a radio node\n   does not distinguish attachments.\"\n  [resolver]\n  (fn attach!\n    [descriptor]\n    (if-not (descriptor? descriptor)\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\n      (if-let [handle (resolver (:dao.stream/identity descriptor))]\n        (if (= (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)\n               (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor handle)))\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n           :dao.stream/handle handle}\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found})\n        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/not-found}))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljd/dao/stream/ble/dart.cljd'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.stream.ble.dart\n  \"The Flutter host seam of docs/design/dao.stream.ble.md 8: one radio\n   node over flutter_blue_plus 1.36.x (BSD-3; the central role -- scan,\n   connect, subscribe, write) and flutter_ble_peripheral (BSD-3; the\n   peripheral role -- advertise, serve GATT, receive writes, notify\n   subscribers). The adapter holds no policy: it maps plugin callbacks\n   into radio events, hands them to the host-free machine\n   dao.stream.ble.links, appends the returned deposits in order,\n   performs the returned actions, and exposes the seam contract\n   {:send! f :close! f} that dao.stream.ble/writer consumes.\n\n   Platform facts this seam classifies rather than hides:\n\n   - A central-side peer address is the remote id the plugin reports --\n     stable per pairing, opaque here. A peripheral-side connection has\n     no address a Flutter plugin exposes: flutter_ble_peripheral's\n     GattWrite carries no central identity, so the whole peripheral\n     role is ONE logical link at one synthetic peer address,\n     `peripheral-peer` -- writes from any subscribed central arrive\n     there, and sendData notifies every subscriber. The mesh's dedup\n     eats the copies; the :max-links ceiling governs central connects.\n   - iOS cannot advertise manufacturer data, so the advertisement\n     carries the service UUID alone and every in-band identity travels\n     in frames.\n   - Every future is caught: a platform refusal the composition should\n     see as data (:bind-failed, :send-fail, :radio-down, :link-failed)\n     is deposited or fed to the machine, never thrown across the seam.\n\n   This seam is explicitly local and non-migratable (datom.world.md,\n   Host Boundaries): it is the one piece of the stack that needs a\n   radio, and it is why the plugin pins are exact -- flutter_blue_plus\n   2.x changed to a license that is not free for for-profit use, and\n   this repository is GPL-2.0-only with a linking exception.\n\n   A function whose body awaits is an async fn on this host and answers\n   a Future; `bind!` itself holds no await and answers the seam at\n   once, the design's two moves intact -- the radio's first facts land\n   on the traffic stream as data whenever the platform answers.\"\n  (:require [\"package:flutter_blue_plus/flutter_blue_plus.dart\" :as fbp]\n            [\"package:flutter_ble_peripheral/flutter_ble_peripheral.dart\" :as periph]\n            [\"dart:async\" :as async]\n            [\"dart:core\" :as core]\n            [dao.stream.ble :as ble]\n            [dao.stream.ble.links :as links])\n  (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List]))\n\n\n(def peripheral-peer\n  \"The one synthetic peer address of the peripheral role: every central\n   that wrote or subscribed, seen through one address the plugin cannot\n   distinguish. Opaque like any other peer address.\"\n  \"peripheral\")\n\n\n(defn- bytes->uint8\n  ^Uint8List [bs]\n  (if (instance? Uint8List bs)\n    bs\n    (Uint8List.fromList (vec bs))))\n\n\n(defn- listen!\n  \"One subscription on a plugin stream, the error path routed: an\n   errored plugin stream is a radio fact, never an unhandled zone\n   event.\"\n  [s on-data on-error]\n  (.listen ^async/Stream s on-data .onError on-error))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The central role (flutter_blue_plus 1.36.x)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn- scan-events!\n  \"Every scan result matching the service UUID is one [:adv peer rssi]\n   radio event. flutter_blue_plus answers each advertisement batch as a\n   list; the machine's discovery throttle owns the repetition.\"\n  [radio!]\n  (listen!\n   fbp/FlutterBluePlus.scanResults\n   (fn [results]\n     (doseq [r (vec results)]\n       (let [^fbp/ScanResult sr r\n             ^fbp/BluetoothDevice d (.-device sr)]\n         (radio! [:adv (.-str (.-remoteId d)) (.-rssi sr)]))))\n   (fn [e] (radio! [:radio-down (str \"scan: \" e)]))))\n\n\n(defn- adapter-events!\n  \"The adapter going off -- unavailable, unauthorized, turning off, off\n   -- is the radio stopping on its own. The stream starts at :unknown\n   and passes through :turningOn on the way up; neither is a stop, and\n   treating them as one tears the node down at bind.\"\n  [radio!]\n  (listen!\n   fbp/FlutterBluePlus.adapterState\n   (fn [state]\n     (when (contains? #{fbp/BluetoothAdapterState.unavailable\n                        fbp/BluetoothAdapterState.unauthorized\n                        fbp/BluetoothAdapterState.turningOff\n                        fbp/BluetoothAdapterState.off}\n                      state)\n       (radio! [:radio-down (str \"adapter state: \" state)])))\n   (fn [e] (radio! [:radio-down (str \"adapter: \" e)]))))\n\n\n(defn- watch-central!\n  \"One connectionState watcher per dialed device, installed once -- the\n   subscription kept in !subs so a stop tears it down: the link going\n   down is a radio event, whatever the cause. Installed only after the\n   connection is live: the stream emits its current value on listen, so\n   a pre-connect watcher would announce a disconnected that never was.\"\n  [radio! !watched !subs peer device]\n  (when-not (contains? @!watched peer)\n    (swap! !watched conj peer)\n    (swap! !subs conj\n           (listen!\n            (.-connectionState ^fbp/BluetoothDevice device)\n            (fn [state]\n              (when (= state fbp/BluetoothConnectionState.disconnected)\n                (radio! [:link-down peer])))\n            (fn [_] (radio! [:link-down peer]))))))\n\n\n(defn- mesh-characteristics\n  \"The mesh service's TX and RX characteristics on `device`,\n   discovered: {:tx c :rx c}, nil until the platform answers, a side\n   nil when the peer does not speak that half of the profile.\"\n  [device service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid]\n  (let [services (await (.discoverServices ^fbp/BluetoothDevice device))\n        ^fbp/BluetoothService svc (some (fn [^fbp/BluetoothService s]\n                                          (when (= (.-str (.-uuid s)) service-uuid) s))\n                                        (vec services))]\n    (when svc\n      {:tx (some (fn [^fbp/BluetoothCharacteristic c]\n                   (when (= (.-str (.-uuid c)) tx-uuid) c))\n                 (vec (.-characteristics svc)))\n       :rx (some (fn [^fbp/BluetoothCharacteristic c]\n                   (when (= (.-str (.-uuid c)) rx-uuid) c))\n                 (vec (.-characteristics svc)))})))\n\n\n(defn- connect-central!\n  \"One central connection to `peer`, per the [:connect ...] action: dial,\n   discover the mesh service, subscribe to its TX characteristic, and\n   route what arrives. Every late fact is a radio event: the\n   connection's frames, its failure, its end. A peer that connects but\n   serves no mesh profile is a failed dial -- the device is released,\n   the dial's slot freed with [:link-failed ...], and the next\n   advertisement window may try again -- never a silent connection the\n   seam holds but cannot use.\"\n  [radio! !links !subs peer service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !watched]\n  (let [release! (fn [d]\n                   (-> (.disconnect ^fbp/BluetoothDevice d)\n                       (.catchError (fn [_] nil))))]\n    (try\n      (let [d (fbp/BluetoothDevice.fromId peer)\n            _ (await (.connect ^fbp/BluetoothDevice d\n                               .timeout (core/Duration .seconds 15)\n                               .autoConnect false))\n            {:keys [tx rx]} (await (mesh-characteristics\n                                    d service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid))]\n        (if tx\n          (do (await (.setNotifyValue ^fbp/BluetoothCharacteristic tx true))\n              (swap! !links assoc peer {:device d :tx tx :rx rx})\n              (watch-central! radio! !watched !subs peer d)\n              (swap! !subs conj\n                     (listen!\n                      (.-onValueReceived ^fbp/BluetoothCharacteristic tx)\n                      (fn [v] (radio! [:frame peer (bytes->uint8 v)]))\n                      (fn [e] (radio! [:send-fail peer (str \"notify: \" e)]))))\n              (radio! [:link-up peer :central]))\n          (do (release! d)\n              (radio! [:link-failed peer]))))\n      (catch Object _\n        (radio! [:link-failed peer])))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The peripheral role (flutter_ble_peripheral)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn- start-peripheral!\n  \"Advertise the service UUID and serve its GATT profile: the TX\n   characteristic notified out, the RX one written in. GattWrite\n   carries no central identity, so every write arrives at the one\n   synthetic peer address. Subscription events are filtered by the TX\n   characteristic: a subscription to a write characteristic is not a\n   mesh link.\"\n  [radio! service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !subs]\n  (let [p (periph/FlutterBlePeripheral)]\n    (try\n      (await (.start ^periph/FlutterBlePeripheral p\n                     .advertiseData\n                     (periph/AdvertiseDataCore .serviceUuid service-uuid)\n                     .gattServer\n                     (periph/GattServerSettings\n                      .serviceUuid service-uuid\n                      .txCharacteristicUuid tx-uuid\n                      .rxCharacteristicUuid rx-uuid)))\n      (swap! !subs conj\n             (listen!\n              (.-onGattWrite ^periph/FlutterBlePeripheral p)\n              (fn [w]\n                (let [^periph/GattWrite gw w]\n                  (when (= (.-characteristicUuid gw) rx-uuid)\n                    (radio! [:frame peripheral-peer\n                             (bytes->uint8 (.-data gw))]))))\n              (fn [e] (radio! [:send-fail peripheral-peer (str \"gatt: \" e)]))))\n      (swap! !subs conj\n             (listen!\n              (.-onCharacteristicSubscriptionChanged\n               ^periph/FlutterBlePeripheral p)\n              (fn [s]\n                (let [^periph/GattSubscription gs s]\n                  (when (= (.-characteristicUuid gs) tx-uuid)\n                    (if (.-subscribed gs)\n                      (radio! [:link-up peripheral-peer :peripheral])\n                      (radio! [:link-down peripheral-peer])))))\n              (fn [_] (radio! [:link-down peripheral-peer]))))\n      (catch Object e\n        (radio! [:bind-failed (str \"peripheral: \" e)])))\n    p))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The seam: bind! and its radio map\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn- perform-connect!\n  \"The [:connect peer] action: dial through the central plugin. A peer\n   the scan never reported is dialed by address anyway -- fromId does\n   not need the advertisement.\"\n  [{:keys [radio! !links !subs !watched service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid]} peer]\n  (connect-central! radio! !links !subs peer service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !watched))\n\n\n(defn- perform-send-frame!\n  \"The [:send-frame peer bytes] action: over the central link's RX\n   characteristic, or -- for the peripheral role's one synthetic peer --\n   sendData, which notifies every subscribed central at once. A failure\n   the host reports later arrives as the send-failed channel of\n   design 4. A link whose peer spoke only half the profile sends\n   nothing: the [:send-fail ...] for a write the host cannot serve is\n   the honest answer, and the mesh's redundancy is the recovery.\"\n  [{:keys [radio! !links tx-uuid]} peer ^Uint8List bs]\n  (if (= peer peripheral-peer)\n    (when-some [p (:peripheral (get @!links peripheral-peer))]\n      (-> (.sendData ^periph/FlutterBlePeripheral p bs\n                     .characteristicUuid tx-uuid)\n          (.catchError\n            (fn [e] (radio! [:send-fail peripheral-peer (str \"send: \" e)])))))\n    (when-some [{:keys [rx]} (get @!links peer)]\n      (when rx\n        (-> (.write ^fbp/BluetoothCharacteristic rx (vec bs) .withoutResponse false)\n            (.catchError\n              (fn [e] (radio! [:send-fail peer (str \"write: \" e)])))))))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- perform-stop-radio!\n  \"The [:stop-radio] action: the scan stops, every central device\n   disconnects, the advertisement stops, and every plugin subscription\n   is cancelled. Idempotent by the plugins' own contracts.\"\n  [{:keys [!links !subs]}]\n  ;; Every teardown future is caught: a platform refusal during stop is\n  ;; nobody's outcome (close! already answered ok), so it is dropped,\n  ;; never an unhandled zone error.\n  (-> (fbp/FlutterBluePlus.stopScan) (.catchError (fn [_] nil)))\n  (doseq [{:keys [device]} (vals @!links)]\n    (when device\n      (-> (.disconnect ^fbp/BluetoothDevice device)\n          (.catchError (fn [_] nil)))))\n  (when-some [p (:peripheral (get @!links peripheral-peer))]\n    (-> (.stop ^periph/FlutterBlePeripheral p)\n        (.catchError (fn [_] nil))))\n  (doseq [s @!subs]\n    (try (.cancel ^async/StreamSubscription s) (catch Object _ nil)))\n  (reset! !links {})\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- start-radio!\n  \"The scan, started once per bind: every advertisement of the service\n   UUID delivered one by one, the machine's discovery throttle owning\n   the repetition; then the peripheral role serving the same profile.\n   The machine is told the radio is up only once both roles answered\n   -- [:radio-up self] is what deposits :bound, the event the design's\n   second move composes the writer from. A platform refusal anywhere is\n   :bind-failed, as data, and no bound is ever deposited.\"\n  [radio! identity service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !subs !links]\n  (try\n    (await (fbp/FlutterBluePlus.startScan\n            .withServices [(fbp/Guid service-uuid)]\n            .continuousUpdates true\n            .oneByOne true))\n    (let [p (start-peripheral! radio! service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !subs)]\n      (swap! !links assoc peripheral-peer {:peripheral p})\n      (radio! [:radio-up identity]))\n    (catch Object e\n      (radio! [:bind-failed (str e)]))))\n\n\n(defn bind!\n  \"The bind move of design 3 over the Flutter plugins: the bind data of\n   section 3 in, the seam contract's {:send! f :close! f} out at once\n   (with :radio! and :state exposed) -- the radio starts underneath,\n   and its first facts (:bound or :bind-failed) arrive on the traffic\n   stream as data. `:now-ms` defaults to the host clock; every other\n   key is composition data with the design's defaults. `:radio!` stays\n   exposed for a composition that must feed the machine platform facts\n   the plugins do not report (a suspension, a permission revocation).\"\n  [{:keys [identity deposit service-uuid max-frame-bytes max-links\n           announce-window-ms now-ms]}]\n  (let [service-uuid (or service-uuid ble/default-service-uuid)\n        tx-uuid ble/default-tx-uuid\n        rx-uuid ble/default-rx-uuid\n        !links (atom {})\n        !watched (atom #{})\n        !subs (atom [])\n        !machine! (atom nil)\n        ;; Forwarding reference: the machine is wired below, but the\n        ;; plugin streams are wired before it answers -- a radio fact\n        ;; arriving in between finds the atom nil and is dropped, and\n        ;; the plugins deliver nothing before their futures resolve.\n        radio! (fn [ev] (when-some [f @!machine!] (f ev)))\n        ctx {:radio! radio! :!links !links :!watched !watched :!subs !subs\n             :service-uuid service-uuid :tx-uuid tx-uuid :rx-uuid rx-uuid}\n        perform! (fn [action]\n                   (let [[kind peer arg] action]\n                     (case kind\n                       :connect (perform-connect! ctx peer)\n                       :send-frame (perform-send-frame! ctx peer (bytes->uint8 arg))\n                       :stop-radio (perform-stop-radio! ctx)\n                       nil)))\n        seam (links/seam\n              {:identity identity\n               :deposit deposit\n               :max-frame-bytes max-frame-bytes\n               :max-links max-links\n               :announce-window-ms announce-window-ms\n               :now-ms (or now-ms\n                           #(.-millisecondsSinceEpoch (core/DateTime.now)))\n               :radio {:perform! perform!}})]\n    (reset! !machine! (:radio! seam))\n    (adapter-events! radio!)\n    (scan-events! radio!)\n    (start-radio! radio! identity service-uuid tx-uuid rx-uuid !subs !links)\n    seam))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream/ble/links.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/ble/mesh.cljc'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream/ble/links.cljc; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/ble/mesh.cljc'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.stream.ble.links\n  \"The host-free link state machine of docs/design/dao.stream.ble.md 6: a\n   seam's judgment is all link bookkeeping, and link bookkeeping is the\n   same on every host, so it lives here -- pure data over radio events --\n   and a host seam is a thin adapter that turns plugin streams into radio\n   events and returned actions into plugin calls. The dart seam holds no\n   policy of its own; a seam cannot drift from the tests that gate this\n   namespace.\n\n   Radio events are host-local vectors (never dao.stream values, never\n   deposited as such):\n\n     [:adv peer rssi]        an advertisement of the service UUID\n     [:link-up peer dir]     one directional GATT link exists\n                             (dir :central connected out, :peripheral in)\n     [:link-down peer]       that directional link is gone\n     [:link-failed peer]     a connect attempt ended without a link\n     [:frame peer bytes]     one frame arrived on that link\n     [:send-fail peer why]   the host reported a queued send as failed\n     [:radio-up self]        the radio started: advertising and scanning\n     [:radio-down why]       the radio stopped on its own (permissions,\n                             bluetooth off, suspension)\n     [:bind-failed why]      the radio never started\n\n   `handle-radio-event` answers {:state s' :deposits [...] :actions [...]}\n   : `:deposits` are dao.stream.ble values the caller appends to the\n   deposit writer in order, `:actions` are the radio calls the policy\n   owns: [:connect peer] -- an advertisement naming a peer with no link\n   and :max-links allowing, the medium's own repetition being the retry\n   cadence -- and nothing for a peer past the ceiling, whose\n   advertisement still deposits peer-seen. The ceiling counts\n   established links and pending dials both, and governs this seam's own\n   dials only: an inbound link always deposits.\n\n   `seam` is the generic adapter, host-free, that composes the machine\n   into the seam contract dao.stream.ble/writer consumes: it takes the\n   bind data and a radio map, appends every deposit to the deposit\n   writer in order, performs every action on the radio, and exposes\n   {:send! f :close! f :radio! f :state f} -- :send!/:close! are the\n   seam contract, :radio! is one radio event in, :state observes the\n   machine for tests. A send crosses the one directional GATT link the\n   peer address names; no link is the clean transport-error of section\n   4 plus a send-failed deposit; and both-directions redundancy -- two\n   nodes that each connect out to the other -- is a BLE fact the mesh's\n   dedup, not the seam's, eats (design 7): the machine cannot correlate\n   two opaque peer addresses, and does not pretend to.\"\n  (:require [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.ble :as ble]))\n\n\n(def directions\n  \"The two roles one node can hold on one GATT link.\"\n  #{:central :peripheral})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The pure state machine\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn initial-state\n  \"The machine's state over the bind data: `:now-ms` is the clock the\n   discovery throttle reads, (f) -> integer milliseconds -- a host seam\n   supplies the host clock, tests supply a deterministic one. The clock\n   is the seam's own operational fact; no protocol state below the seam\n   reads it.\"\n  [{:keys [identity max-links announce-window-ms max-frame-bytes now-ms]}]\n  (when-not (ble/node-id? identity)\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble seam needs :identity, a node id\"\n                    {:identity identity})))\n  (when-not (fn? now-ms)\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble seam needs :now-ms, a clock\"\n                    {:now-ms now-ms})))\n  {:identity identity\n   :max-links (or max-links ble/default-max-links)\n   :announce-window-ms (or announce-window-ms\n                           ble/default-announce-window-ms)\n   :max-frame-bytes (or max-frame-bytes ble/default-max-frame-bytes)\n   :now-ms now-ms\n   :links {}\n   :connecting #{}\n   :announced {}\n   :closed? false})\n\n\n(defn- link-direction\n  \"The direction last reported for the link `peer` names. One peer\n   address is one entry: a platform that reports both directions of one\n   pairing under one address re-marks the entry's direction, and the\n   first [:link-down ...] for that address drops it.\"\n  [state peer]\n  (:direction (get (:links state) peer)))\n\n\n(defn- throttle-deposit?\n  \"True iff `state` owes `peer` a peer-seen deposit now: no window is\n   open, or its window has elapsed. The first advertisement always\n   deposits.\"\n  [state peer]\n  (if-some [since (get (:announced state) peer)]\n    (>= ((:now-ms state)) (+ since (:announce-window-ms state)))\n    true))\n\n\n(defn- note-announced\n  [state peer]\n  (assoc-in state [:announced peer] ((:now-ms state))))\n\n\n(defn- link-count-ok?\n  \"The ceiling counts established links and pending connects both: a\n   burst of advertisements from different peers issues at most\n   :max-links dials, whatever the radio does with them.\"\n  [state]\n  (< (+ (count (:links state)) (count (:connecting state)))\n     (:max-links state)))\n\n\n(defn- prune-announced\n  \"Announcement windows older than :announce-window-ms are dropped, so\n   a scanner seeing rotating random addresses does not grow the map\n   without bound. Pure: answers the pruned state.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [now ((:now-ms state))\n        horizon (- now (:announce-window-ms state))]\n    (assoc state :announced\n           (into {} (filter (fn [[_ since]] (>= since horizon)))\n                 (:announced state)))))\n\n\n(defn- answer\n  \"The machine's answer shape: the next state, the deposits to append in\n   order, the actions to perform in order.\"\n  [state deposits actions]\n  {:state state :deposits (vec deposits) :actions (vec actions)})\n\n\n(defn handle-radio-event\n  \"One radio event in, the machine's answer out. Total: an event the\n   machine does not know is answered with the state unchanged, no\n   deposits, no actions -- a newer radio, not a defect.\"\n  [state ev]\n  (let [[kind peer arg] ev]\n    (if (:closed? state)\n      (answer state [] [])\n      (case kind\n        :radio-up\n        (answer state [(ble/bound-event (:identity state) peer)] [])\n\n        :bind-failed\n        (answer state [(ble/bind-failed-event (:identity state) peer)] [])\n\n        :radio-down\n        ;; The radio stopped on its own: every live link is lost, and the\n        ;; node this composition held is observably gone (dao.stream.md,\n        ;; Surfaces). Sends through the writer answer transport-error\n        ;; from here on -- no links -- until the composition re-binds.\n        (let [peers (vec (keys (:links state)))]\n          (answer (assoc state :links {} :connecting #{} :announced {})\n                  (into [(ble/closed-event (:identity state))]\n                        (map (fn [p]\n                               (ble/link-down-event (:identity state) p)))\n                        peers)\n                  [[:stop-radio]]))\n\n        :adv\n        ;; One observed advertisement of the service UUID. The throttle\n        ;; owes at most one peer-seen per peer per :announce-window-ms,\n        ;; latest RSSI winning; the connect policy rides the same window,\n        ;; so the medium's own repetition is the retry cadence.\n        (let [s (prune-announced state)\n              owes? (throttle-deposit? s peer)\n              s (if owes? (note-announced s peer) s)\n              deposits (if owes?\n                         [(ble/peer-seen-event (:identity state) peer arg)]\n                         [])\n              actions (if (and owes?\n                               (not (contains? (:links state) peer))\n                               (not (contains? (:connecting s) peer))\n                               (link-count-ok? s))\n                        [[:connect peer]]\n                        [])\n              s (if (seq actions)\n                  (update s :connecting conj peer)\n                  s)]\n          (answer s deposits actions))\n\n        ;; The ceiling of section 6 governs this seam's own dials, not\n        ;; what the OS accepts on its side: an inbound link always\n        ;; deposits, whatever :max-links says.\n        :link-up\n        (answer (-> state\n                    (assoc-in [:links peer] {:direction arg})\n                    (update :connecting disj peer))\n                [(ble/link-up-event (:identity state) peer arg)]\n                [])\n\n        ;; A link whose peer address the machine never saw come up is a\n        ;; radio fact about nothing this node holds -- the stale\n        ;; duplicate of a teardown already processed. It deposits\n        ;; nothing: a never-held link is not a lost one.\n        :link-down\n        (if (contains? (:links state) peer)\n          (answer (-> state\n                      (update :links dissoc peer)\n                      (update :connecting disj peer))\n                  [(ble/link-down-event (:identity state) peer)\n                   (ble/peer-lost-event (:identity state) peer)]\n                  [])\n          (answer (update state :connecting disj peer) [] []))\n\n        ;; A connect attempt ended without a link: no deposit -- the next\n        ;; advertisement window retries, and a never-up link is not a\n        ;; lost one.\n        :link-failed\n        (answer (update state :connecting disj peer) [] [])\n\n        :frame\n        ;; The :max-frame-bytes drop is protocol validation below the\n        ;; transform: an inbound frame over the budget is never\n        ;; deposited, the same symmetric budget design 4 refuses at\n        ;; append!.\n        (if (> (bytes/length arg) (:max-frame-bytes state))\n          (answer state [] [])\n          (answer state\n                  [(ble/frame-event (:identity state) peer arg)]\n                  []))\n\n        :send-fail\n        (answer state\n                [(ble/send-failed-event (:identity state) peer arg)]\n                [])\n\n        (answer state [] [])))))\n\n\n(defn send!\n  \"One frame toward `peer`, decoded host bytes, over the one directional\n   GATT link that peer address names: either an outcome and a\n   [:send-frame ...] action, or the clean transport-error of section 4\n   plus a send-failed deposit when no such link exists at this moment.\n   \\\"No link\\\" is a normal BLE fact -- links come and go with proximity\n   -- and is not distinguished from any other failure; the writer of a\n   flooding mesh does not retry it, because the mesh's redundancy is the\n   retry.\"\n  [state peer bs]\n  (cond\n    (:closed? state)\n    {:state state\n     :outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n     :deposits [] :actions []}\n\n    (not (ble/peer-address? peer))\n    {:state state\n     :outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n               :dao.stream.ble/reason \"malformed destination\"}\n     :deposits [(ble/send-failed-event (:identity state) peer\n                                       \"malformed destination\")]\n     :actions []}\n\n    (nil? (link-direction state peer))\n    {:state state\n     :outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n               :dao.stream.ble/reason \"no link to peer\"}\n     :deposits [(ble/send-failed-event (:identity state) peer\n                                       \"no link to peer\")]\n     :actions []}\n\n    :else\n    {:state state\n     :outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n     :deposits []\n     :actions [[:send-frame peer bs]]}))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"The close path: the node stops advertising, scanning and serving, and\n   is observably gone. Every live link is lost with it, and each is\n   deposited as a link-down first -- the mesh above learns its neighbors\n   from those events, and a link fact the reader held must not outlive\n   the reader's evidence for it (design 5). Idempotent: a closed node's\n   close answers without deposits or actions.\"\n  [state]\n  (if (:closed? state)\n    (answer state [] [])\n    (answer (assoc state :closed? true :links {} :connecting #{})\n            (into [(ble/closed-event (:identity state))]\n                  (map (fn [p] (ble/link-down-event (:identity state) p)))\n                  (keys (:links state)))\n            [[:stop-radio]])))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The generic adapter: one seam, any radio\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn- append-deposits!\n  \"Every deposit onto the deposit writer, in order, each appended even if\n   the one before it answered non-ok -- the deposit writer is an evicting\n   ring by contract and never refuses (design 5). Total even if the\n   composition behind it is broken.\"\n  [deposit deposits]\n  (doseq [ev deposits]\n    (try (stream/append! deposit ev)\n         (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil))))\n\n\n(defn seam\n  \"The generic adapter (design 6): the machine composed into the seam\n   contract `dao.stream.ble/writer` consumes, over a `radio` map the\n   host supplies:\n\n     {:perform! (fn [action])}   machine -> radio: [:connect peer],\n                                 [:send-frame peer bytes], [:stop-radio]\n\n   and answers {:send! f :close! f :radio! f :state f}: `:send!`/\n   `:close!` are the seam contract (bind data in, those two out, deposits\n   flowing to `:deposit` only); `:radio!` is the adapter's one entry, one\n   radio event in; `:state` observes the machine, for tests. `:send!`\n   hands the frame to the radio and answers `ok` -- it asserts nothing\n   about the air -- and a failure the host reports later arrives as a\n   [:send-fail ...] radio event, the send-failed channel of design 4.\n   The radio's own send failures are deposited; they are not retried.\"\n  [{:keys [identity deposit max-frame-bytes max-links announce-window-ms\n           now-ms radio]}]\n  (when-not (and (map? radio) (fn? (:perform! radio)))\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble seam needs a radio with :perform!\"\n                    {:radio radio})))\n  (let [state (atom (initial-state\n                     {:identity identity\n                      :max-links max-links\n                      :announce-window-ms announce-window-ms\n                      :max-frame-bytes max-frame-bytes\n                      :now-ms now-ms}))\n        run! (fn [a]\n               (reset! state (:state a))\n               (append-deposits! deposit (:deposits a))\n               (doseq [action (:actions a)] ((:perform! radio) action)))]\n    {:send!\n     (fn [peer bs]\n       (let [a (send! @state peer bs)]\n         (run! a)\n         (:outcome a)))\n     :close!\n     (fn []\n       (run! (close! @state))\n       nil)\n     :radio!\n     (fn [ev]\n       (run! (handle-radio-event @state ev))\n       nil)\n     :state\n     (fn [] @state)}))\n(ns dao.stream.ble.mesh\n  \"The flooding mesh of docs/design/dao.stream.ble.md 7: the value channel\n   over the raw BLE layer, what dao.stream.udp is to the datagram layer.\n\n   One CBOR value, canonical profile (dao.stream.cbor), rides one mesh\n   frame, which rides one raw frame -- no fragmentation in S1; an encoded\n   value over the raw budget is refused at send! as the raw writer's\n   invalid-value. A mesh frame is a CBOR map keyed under\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/ whose :v key is the discriminator this layer\n   owns on a shared radio (dao.stream.datagram.md 6): the mesh owns\n   exactly the raw frames whose decoded value is a map carrying it, and\n   drops maps carrying no such key. A frame with the key but malformed\n   fields is foreign the same way -- a newer or broken dialect, not a\n   defect to diagnose on any shared stream.\n\n   Routing is flooding and flooding is all of it. A received frame whose\n   [origin seq] is new is remembered (bounded, oldest evicted), delivered\n   locally, and relayed with ttl decremented to every current neighbor\n   except the source peer; a frame remembered or at ttl 0 delivers only\n   the first thing or nothing. The neighbor set is the raw layer's, read\n   from the traffic stream's link-up/link-down lifecycle events as the\n   mesh steps. There is no route computation: a peer address is opaque\n   and link-scoped (dao.stream.ble.md 2), so no route table above the\n   raw layer could be honest. Flooding tolerates loss: a failed relay is\n   a lost frame, never retried here, the raw traffic stream's send-failed\n   events being the data a composition that wants to observe loss\n   observes.\n\n   Deduplication is bounded, not timely: :max-remembered [origin seq]\n   keys, oldest evicted, no clock, no freshness judgment. A replayed old\n   sequence outside the cache window is re-delivered and re-relayed, and\n   idempotence of the payload is the payload's -- the same rule\n   dao.stream.md (Writing) states for retries.\n\n   The mesh value, `make-mesh`, is an atom-backed protocol state, the\n   dao.stream.udp port shape. The composition drives `mesh-step!` at its\n   cadence, one owner per mesh; sends answer immediately what is true\n   now (dao.stream.md, The IO Model) -- a send before the first step\n   floods to zero neighbors and answers ok, the cadence being the\n   composition's.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.base64 :as base64]\n            [dao.stream.ble :as ble]\n            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]))\n\n\n(def version\n  \"The mesh dialect this namespace speaks.\"\n  1)\n\n\n(def default-ttl\n  \"The default hop budget of an origin send: six relays is three to five\n   hops of margin around the Bluetooth Mesh default without the\n   thundering herd a large ttl makes of a dense mesh.\"\n  6)\n\n\n(def default-max-remembered\n  \"The default dedup cache bound, in [origin seq] keys, oldest evicted\n   first.\"\n  4096)\n\n\n(def default-step-budget\n  \"The default mesh-step! budget: raw events consumed in one step when\n   the composition does not name one.\"\n  64)\n\n\n(def max-frame-bytes-default\n  \"The raw budget a composition that names none is expected to bind its\n   seam with. The mesh does not enforce it -- the raw layer does, both\n   ways -- and carries no copy of it; send! answers whatever the raw\n   writer answers for an oversize value: invalid-value.\"\n  ble/default-max-frame-bytes)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Frames and deposits (section 7)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn frame?\n  \"True iff `m` is one of this layer's frames: the discriminator at this\n   dialect's version, an origin identity, a non-negative seq and ttl,\n   and a payload value the CBOR codec already admitted (decode\n   guarantees it; a hand-built frame is judged by the same fields).\"\n  [m]\n  (and (map? m)\n       (= version (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/v m))\n       (string? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin m))\n       (pos? (count (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin m)))\n       (integer? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq m))\n       (not (neg? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq m)))\n       (integer? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/ttl m))\n       (not (neg? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/ttl m)))))\n\n\n(defn frame\n  \"One mesh frame over `origin`, `seq`, `ttl` and the payload `value`.\"\n  [origin seq ttl value]\n  {:dao.stream.ble.mesh/v version\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin origin\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq seq\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/ttl ttl\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/value value})\n\n\n(defn delivery-event\n  \"One mesh value delivered to this node: the payload, its origin and\n   seq for correlation, and the source peer the frame arrived on.\"\n  [peer origin seq value]\n  {:dao.stream.ble.mesh/source peer\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin origin\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq seq\n   :dao.stream.ble.mesh/value value})\n\n\n(defn delivery-event?\n  \"True iff `v` is one local delivery of section 7. Observers of a mesh's\n   deposit medium judge every value with this.\"\n  [v]\n  (and (map? v)\n       (string? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/source v))\n       (pos? (count (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/source v)))\n       (string? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin v))\n       (pos? (count (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin v)))\n       (integer? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq v))\n       (not (neg? (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq v)))))\n\n\n(defn- decode-frame\n  \"The frame one raw frame event carries, or nil when it is not this\n   layer's: not strict Base64 (the raw layer would never deposit that),\n   not decodable CBOR, or not a frame of section 7. A foreign or\n   malformed frame is skipped, never answered, never diagnosed on any\n   shared stream.\"\n  [text]\n  (when-let [bs (base64/decode text)]\n    (try\n      (let [m (cbor/decode bs)]\n        (when (frame? m) m))\n      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n        nil))))\n\n\n(defn- encode-frame\n  \"The Base64 of `frame`'s canonical CBOR, or nil when the frame is\n   outside the portable domain -- a refusal the raw writer never has to\n   see.\"\n  [m]\n  (try\n    (base64/encode (cbor/encode m))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n      nil)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The mesh value\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(defn make-mesh\n  \"The mesh over one bound raw node: `:raw-writer` is the node's writer\n   handle (dao.stream.ble/writer), `:raw-traffic` its traffic ring,\n   `:raw-cursor` the cursor this mesh reads it from -- the composition's\n   choice of :oldest (replay what the ring still holds) or :newest (live\n   only); `:deposit` is the mesh's own deposit medium, the composition\n   wires an evicting ring for the datagram reasons (design 7).\n   `:identity` is the origin identity frames carry, composition minted,\n   unauthenticated, whose only office is loop prevention and\n   correlation. `:ttl` bounds a send's hops, `:max-remembered` bounds\n   the dedup cache.\"\n  [{:keys [identity raw-writer raw-traffic raw-cursor deposit\n           ttl max-remembered]}]\n  (when-not (and (string? identity) (pos? (count identity)))\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble mesh needs :identity, its origin\"\n                    {:identity identity})))\n  (when-not (stream/writer? raw-writer)\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble mesh needs :raw-writer, the node's writer handle\"\n                    {:raw-writer raw-writer})))\n  (when-not (stream/reader? raw-traffic)\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble mesh needs :raw-traffic, the node's traffic ring\"\n                    {:raw-traffic raw-traffic})))\n  (when-not (stream/writer? deposit)\n    (throw (ex-info \"a dao.stream.ble mesh needs :deposit, an evicting ring the composition wires\"\n                    {:deposit deposit})))\n  (let [c (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor raw-traffic raw-cursor))]\n    (when-not c\n      (throw (ex-info \"the raw traffic ring refused :raw-cursor\"\n                      {:raw-cursor raw-cursor})))\n    (atom {:identity identity\n           :raw-writer raw-writer\n           :raw-traffic raw-traffic\n           :raw-cursor c\n           :deposit deposit\n           :ttl (or ttl default-ttl)\n           :max-remembered (or max-remembered default-max-remembered)\n           :next-seq 0\n           :neighbors #{}\n           :seen {}\n           :seen-queue []})))\n\n\n(defn- remember\n  \"One [origin seq] key remembered: into the set and the eviction queue,\n   oldest evicted past :max-remembered.\"\n  [s k]\n  (let [seen (assoc (:seen s) k true)\n        q (conj (:seen-queue s) k)\n        over (- (count q) (:max-remembered s))]\n    (if (pos? over)\n      (let [dropped (subvec q 0 over)]\n        (assoc s\n               :seen (apply dissoc seen dropped)\n               :seen-queue (subvec q over)))\n      (assoc s :seen seen :seen-queue q))))\n\n\n(defn- fan-out!\n  \"One frame's Base64 appended toward EVERY neighbor in `peers` through\n   the raw writer -- every append happens, whatever the one before it\n   answered: a lazy sequence here would let one stale neighbor silently\n   skip the append to every neighbor after it, and the loss of a flood\n   is exactly what flooding's redundancy is not allowed to multiply.\n   Answers {:outcomes [...] :first-refusal r} -- one outcome per peer in\n   order, and the first non-ok outcome among them, nil when every append\n   answered ok. The send-failed events on the raw traffic ring are the\n   loss record; flooding tolerates loss.\"\n  [raw-writer peers b64]\n  (let [outcomes (mapv (fn [peer]\n                         (stream/append!\n                          raw-writer\n                          {:dao.stream.ble/destination peer\n                           :dao.stream.ble/bytes b64}))\n                       peers)\n        first-refusal (some (fn [r]\n                              (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n                                r))\n                            outcomes)]\n    {:outcomes outcomes :first-refusal first-refusal}))\n\n\n(defn send!\n  \"One origin send: `value` encoded canonical CBOR, wrapped in a frame\n   with this mesh's origin, its next seq and its configured ttl, fanned\n   out to every current neighbor. Answers the raw writer's first non-ok\n   outcome -- an unencodable value is refused as invalid-value before\n   the raw writer is touched, nothing was appended -- and ok when every\n   append answered ok, which asserts nothing about the air. The seq\n   advances when at least one link accepted the frame: a seq no link\n   accepted was heard by nobody, and reusing it is safe; a seq any link\n   accepted is spent forever, for the dedup cache's sake.\"\n  [mesh value]\n  (let [{:keys [identity raw-writer ttl neighbors next-seq]} @mesh\n        f (frame identity next-seq ttl value)]\n    (if-some [b64 (encode-frame f)]\n      (if (empty? neighbors)\n        ;; Nobody to flood to is not a refusal: the frame crossed no\n        ;; link, the seq stays unspent, and the composition's cadence\n        ;; drives when links have been learned.\n        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n        (let [{:keys [outcomes first-refusal]}\n              (fan-out! raw-writer neighbors b64)\n              accepted? (some #(= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome %))\n                              outcomes)]\n          (when accepted?\n            ;; The seq is spent when at least one link accepted the\n            ;; frame, and the spend is remembered: this node's own send\n            ;; occupies the dedup cache exactly as a received frame\n            ;; would, so the copy a redundant link brings back delivers\n            ;; nothing and relays nothing. A seq any link accepted is\n            ;; spent forever -- the node that accepted the first copy\n            ;; would dedup a reused seq's second value away -- and a seq\n            ;; no link accepted is forgotten by everyone, ourselves\n            ;; included.\n            (swap! mesh (fn [s]\n                          (-> s\n                              (assoc :next-seq (inc next-seq))\n                              (remember [identity next-seq])))))\n          (or first-refusal {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})))\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value\n       :dao.stream.ble.mesh/reason \"value is outside the portable domain\"})))\n\n\n(defn- step-event!\n  \"One raw traffic event consumed into the mesh: lifecycle events keep\n   the neighbor set current, frame events deliver and relay. Answers\n   {:delivered n :relayed n}, zeros for a skipped event.\"\n  [mesh v]\n  (cond\n    (ble/lifecycle-event? v)\n    (do (case (:dao.stream.ble/event v)\n          :dao.stream.ble/link-up\n          (swap! mesh update :neighbors conj (:dao.stream.ble/peer v))\n          :dao.stream.ble/link-down\n          (swap! mesh update :neighbors disj (:dao.stream.ble/peer v))\n          :dao.stream.ble/peer-lost\n          (swap! mesh update :neighbors disj (:dao.stream.ble/peer v))\n          ;; The raw layer says the node itself is gone: every link fact\n          ;; this mesh held is stale, the same rule a gap gets below.\n          :dao.stream.ble/closed\n          (swap! mesh assoc :neighbors #{})\n          nil)\n        {:delivered 0 :relayed 0})\n\n    (ble/frame-event? v)\n    (let [source (:dao.stream.ble/source v)\n          f (decode-frame (:dao.stream.ble/bytes v))]\n      (if-not f\n        {:delivered 0 :relayed 0}\n        (let [origin (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/origin f)\n              sq (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/seq f)\n              k [origin sq]]\n          (if (contains? (:seen @mesh) k)\n            {:delivered 0 :relayed 0}\n            (do (swap! mesh remember k)\n                (try (stream/append!\n                      (:deposit @mesh)\n                      (delivery-event source origin sq\n                                      (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/value f)))\n                     (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n                       nil))\n                (let [ttl (min (:dao.stream.ble.mesh/ttl f) (:ttl @mesh))]\n                  ;; A wire ttl above this mesh's own budget is clamped\n                  ;; to it: amplification is bounded by the configuration\n                  ;; this composition chose, not by what a frame claims.\n                  (if-not (pos? ttl)\n                    {:delivered 1 :relayed 0}\n                    (let [b64 (encode-frame\n                               (assoc f :dao.stream.ble.mesh/ttl (dec ttl)))\n                          r (when b64\n                              (fan-out! (:raw-writer @mesh)\n                                        (remove #(= source %) (:neighbors @mesh))\n                                        b64))]\n                      ;; A failed relay is a lost frame, never retried\n                      ;; here; the refusal is dropped, the raw\n                      ;; send-failed events being the loss record.\n                      {:delivered 1\n                       :relayed (cond\n                                  (not b64) 0\n                                  (some #(= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome %))\n                                        (:outcomes r)) 1\n                                  :else 0)}))))))))\n\n    :else\n    ;; Neither this layer's: another interpreter's value, or a host fact\n    ;; no rule here -- skipped, never diagnosed.\n    {:delivered 0 :relayed 0}))\n\n\n(defn mesh-step!\n  \"One step reading the raw traffic ring into the mesh: read to blocked\n   from the mesh's own raw cursor, bounded by `budget` events, keep the\n   neighbor set current from lifecycle events, deliver and relay owned\n   frames. A raw gap adopts the recovery cursor and the step continues --\n   those events are lost, indistinguishable from radio loss, and\n   flooding's redundancy recovers exactly as it does for network loss.\n   Answers {:processed n :delivered n :relayed n :status :drained |\n   :budget}. One owner per mesh drives this, at the composition's\n   cadence.\"\n  ([mesh] (mesh-step! mesh default-step-budget))\n  ([mesh budget]\n   (let [{:keys [raw-traffic]} @mesh\n         stop (volatile! nil)\n         counts (volatile! {:processed 0 :delivered 0 :relayed 0})]\n     (loop [left budget]\n       (when (pos? left)\n         (let [r (stream/next raw-traffic (:raw-cursor @mesh))]\n           (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n             :dao.stream/ok\n             (do (vswap! counts\n                         (fn [c]\n                           (let [{:keys [delivered relayed]}\n                                 (step-event! mesh (:dao.stream/value r))]\n                             (-> c\n                                 (update :processed inc)\n                                 (update :delivered + delivered)\n                                 (update :relayed + relayed)))))\n                 (swap! mesh assoc :raw-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n                 (recur (dec left)))\n\n             :dao.stream/blocked\n             (vreset! stop :drained)\n\n             :dao.stream/gap\n             ;; A raw gap adopts the recovery cursor and the step\n             ;; continues: those events are lost, indistinguishable\n             ;; from radio loss, and flooding's redundancy recovers\n             ;; exactly as it does for network loss. Link facts the\n             ;; gap swallowed may leave the neighbor set stale -- a\n             ;; dead neighbor's relays answer send-failed and cost\n             ;; nothing, while a cleared set would cost the mesh every\n             ;; relay it has, and a stable link emits no link-up to\n             ;; refill it. Stale-but-usable is the honest choice, and\n             ;; the next link-down corrects it.\n             (do (swap! mesh assoc :raw-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n                 (recur (dec left)))\n\n             ;; A closed or failed raw ring stops the step; the mesh\n             ;; answers what it processed. The composition re-binds or\n             ;; tears down; this layer invents neither.\n             (vreset! stop :drained)))))\n     (assoc @counts :status (or @stop :budget)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
